            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)=\\sqrt{x^3+x^2-8}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p54-q53-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f^{-1}$ exists.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is strictly increasing on its natural domain, so it is one-to-one and $f^{-1}$ exists.",
          "worked": "For $x\\leq0$, $x^3+x^2\\leq\\frac{4}{27}<8$, and for $0\\leq x\\leq1$, $x^3+x^2\\leq2<8$. Hence every point in the domain satisfies $x>1$.\n\nFor $x>1$, the radicand is strictly increasing, since\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(x^3+x^2-8)=x(3x+2)>0.$$\nIt therefore has exactly one zero $\\alpha\\in(1,2)$, and the domain of $f$ is $[\\alpha,\\infty)$.\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{x(3x+2)}{2\\sqrt{x^3+x^2-8}}>0\\qquad(x>\\alpha).$$\nAlso, $f(\\alpha)=0<f(x)$ for $x>\\alpha$. Thus $f$ is strictly increasing on its entire domain, so it is one-to-one and $f^{-1}$ exists.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q53-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $f(f(x))=x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p54-q53-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=2",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p54-q53-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Strict increase rules out a two-cycle, so solve ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Since $f$ is strictly increasing, $f(x)>x$ would give $f(f(x))>f(x)>x$, while $f(x)<x$ would give $f(f(x))<f(x)<x$. Therefore $f(f(x))=x$ requires $f(x)=x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{x^3+x^2-8}&=x\\\\x^3+x^2-8&=x^2\\\\x^3&=8\\\\x&=2\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $f(2)=\\sqrt{8+4-8}=2$, so $f(f(2))=2$. Thus $x=2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
